# dosfstools -- mkfs.fat. UEFI firmware boots from a FAT32 EFI system partition
# and reads nothing else on a fixed disk, so the graphical installer cannot
# create a bootable disk without this. It is the second of the two packages
# that stood between Duct and installing itself.
#
# fsck.fat matters too, though later: the installer writes an fstab giving the
# ESP a pass number of 2, so the installed system checks it at boot.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/dosfstools/dosfstools/releases/download/v4.2/dosfstools-4.2.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=64926eebf90092dca21b14259a5301b7b98e7b1943e8a201c7d726084809b527
SRC_DIR=dosfstools-4.2

# --enable-compat-symlinks IS LOAD-BEARING AND ITS DEFAULT IS OFF.
#
# configure.ac:29 sets symlinks=false when the flag is absent, and the package
# then installs exactly three programs: mkfs.fat, fsck.fat and fatlabel. The
# name every piece of documentation uses, and the name the Duct installer
# invokes, is MKFS.VFAT -- which is one of the compat symlinks. Without this
# flag the package installs successfully, contains a working FAT formatter, and
# the installer fails at the point of creating the EFI system partition with
# "mkfs.vfat: not found".
#
# The symlinks land in sbindir (src/Makefile.am:51-58), so they are
# /usr/sbin/mkfs.vfat and friends. install.sh asserts the one the installer
# depends on, rather than trusting this flag to have been honoured.
#
# Two flags this recipe deliberately does NOT pass, because a flag that does
# nothing is worse than no flag -- it reads as a decision and cannot be tested:
#
#   --disable-nls    dosfstools has no NLS support at all. There is no
#                    AM_GNU_GETTEXT in configure.ac and no --disable-nls in the
#                    generated configure, so passing it would produce
#                    "unrecognized options" and change nothing. Every other
#                    recipe here passes it because every other package has
#                    gettext; this one does not.
#
#   --sysconfdir     nothing here installs into /etc. The flag is required for
#                    autotools packages that do -- autotools, unlike meson,
#                    does not special-case a /usr prefix -- and this package
#                    installs three programs, their symlinks and their manuals,
#                    and nothing else.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--enable-compat-symlinks"
